{"name":"Acorn","hex":"#7f5e50","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acorn","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit of the oak, being an oval nut growing in a woody cup or cupule.","A cone-shaped piece of wood on the point of the spindle above the vane, on the mast-head."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acorn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acorn","blocks":[{"text":"The acorn is the nut of the oaks and their close relatives ; alternatively known as glans. It usually contains a seedling surrounded by two cotyledons, enclosed in a tough shell known as the pericarp, and borne in a cup-shaped cupule. Acorns take between 5 and 24 months to mature; see the list of Quercus species for details of oak classification, in which acorn morphology and phenology are important factors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorn","wikidata":"Q3914781"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg/500px-Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg/1280px-Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg/500px-Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"English oak acorn","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Acorns_small_to_large.jpg/500px-Acorns_small_to_large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campai